<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">The core concept — Event Stream and Knowledge State</span>
    GitLab is the <strong>Event Stream</strong> of development changes, and the Wiki is the <strong>Knowledge State</strong> that is valid at the present moment. Commits, MRs, and Releases keep piling up in GitLab in time order, but the Wiki is not a list of history like "changes on 7/1, changes on 7/2…" — it is refined and maintained as the <strong>knowledge structure the organization needs to know right now</strong> (Architecture · Rule Engine · state systems · channel policies · Troubleshooting …).
</div>

<h3>What Knowledge State Really Is — A Deterministically Built Index</h3>
<p>"The currently valid state of knowledge" must not be an abstract slogan but a <strong>mechanically reproducible artifact</strong>. The Reference Wiki has already demonstrated this: the metadata of every document (title · summary · concepts · relations) becomes a knowledge index JSON through a <a href="#!kgs-overview">deterministic build</a> from a single curation file, and <strong>the same input always yields the same output</strong>. That is why a single <code>--check</code> can decide, byte for byte, whether "the stored state has diverged from the source", and a merge is impossible while it has diverged (<a href="#!kgs-gates">drift gate</a>). A structure where <strong>a machine, not discipline</strong>, prevents the Knowledge State from falling behind the Event Stream or contradicting the source — this is the substance of the state management the company platform will inherit.</p>

<h3>The Road the Industry Is Already On</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Documents next to the code</strong> — Spotify runs more than 5,000 documentation sites with <a href="https://backstage.io/blog/2020/09/08/announcing-tech-docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Backstage TechDocs</a>, which keeps documents in the same repository as the code and builds them in CI, and GitLab is famous for the <a href="https://handbook.gitlab.com/handbook/company/culture/all-remote/handbook-first/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">handbook-first culture</a> of its public handbook of more than 2,700 pages. docs-as-code is an approach already proven in large organizations.</li>
    <li><strong>Automating document synchronization</strong> — <a href="https://swimm.io/blog/how-does-swimm-s-auto-sync-feature-work" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Swimm</a> commercialized "Continuous Documentation" with an Auto-sync that compares code changes against documents on every PR and updates them automatically.</li>
    <li><strong>Automatic knowledge extraction from code</strong> — Cognition's <a href="https://cognition.com/blog/deepwiki" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DeepWiki (2025)</a> analyzes a repository and automatically generates a wiki containing architecture diagrams and module documents, and has indexed more than 50,000 top public repositories. GitHub made <a href="https://github.blog/changelog/2025-09-24-copilot-spaces-is-now-generally-available/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copilot Spaces</a>, which injects repository knowledge at all times, generally available, and Claude Code's <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CLAUDE.md</a> and AGENTS.md turned "machine-read knowledge files inside the repository" into an industry convention.</li>
    <li><strong>Rapid growth of the knowledge search market</strong> — <a href="https://futurumgroup.com/insights/glean-doubles-arr-to-200m-can-its-knowledge-graph-beat-copilot/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Glean, an enterprise-wide AI knowledge search, passed $200M in ARR</a>, and Atlassian bundled <a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/rovo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rovo</a> into its cloud plans by default.</li>
</ul>
<p>In short, <strong>"AI-driven automatic knowledge generation from GitLab development results" is the application of automatic extraction technology commercialized since 2025 to organizational knowledge management, on top of the proven foundation of docs-as-code</strong>.</p>
